**2. Reverse shell upload**
Based on the Framework and version and looking at the syn/ ack packets sent it was decided to look for CVE's based authorisation bypass weaknesses in the javascript framework. This led to identification of a weakness using the x-middleware-subrequest header and and creating a reverse shell using Netcat, redirecting the target to ip: 113.89.232.157. and port 31337: Identifying framework: [view screenshot](../screenshots/js%20+%20version.png), Searching CVE's: [view screenshots](../screenshots/searching%20for%20CVE.png) Finding CVE used: [view screenshot](../screenshots/Idenitfying%20correct%20CVE.png) Finding weaknesses: [view screenshot](../screenshots/confirming%20malicious%20ip.png)
**3. URI analysis**
To determine how the attacker gained access to the system it was necessary to review the uris. I searched for uris accessed by the attacker then reviewed them methodically, looking at each event until something stood out. /admin and /api/upload showed the CVE weakness in use but they were not the correct answers in this case. It was then I decided to site exploration. Focusing on robots.txt and sitemap.xml, as these publicly accessible discovery files often expose hidden or restricted application paths that attackers can abuse for reconnaissance: Finding unique uris accessed: [view screenshot](../screenshots/finding%20unique%20uris%20accessed.png) uri analysis: [view screenshot](../screenshots/uri%20analysis.png) checking robots.txt: [view screenshot](../screenshots/verify%202%20uri's.png)
